Feature: The matcher's procedure
  How the matcher judges records the same: which records it takes, in what order, by which rules, and what it writes.

  Each pod starts empty, at https://pod.example/, with one person as its subject (scripted-input/people.ttl). Cleo
  claims her hospital profile with an About, imports five allergies, enters ten more by hand, and judges some herself.
  Dev enters pairs of allergies, procedures and flu shots for the matcher to compare, and later two more allergies.
  Eve enters ten allergies whose codes the ingredient map pairs, and retracts or replaces some of the matcher's
  Sames, and once retracts her own supersession. Finn brings exports and entries a runtime must refuse, and two
  allergies of one code, and, with the rule list of an app, connects to a hospital's server at its address and again at
  a new path or a new host.
  Gus enters medications and lab results, and three flu shots, under the rule list's version 2.
  Hana enters nine conditions, whose codes recur, under the rule list's version 4. Gus, with the drug tables of an
  app, enters seven medications of RxNorm and NDC codes under the rule list's version 6. Gus, with the drug tables and
  the drug products table of an app, enters pairs of medications that share an NDC, a product code or only an
  ingredient, each pair in an entry of its own, under the rule list's version 7. Ivo, with the variant classifications
  table of an app, enters pairs of variants that carry a ClinVar ID, an HGVS expression or both, each pair in an entry
  of its own, under the rule list's version 8. Gus, with the vocabulary's rule list of an app, enters lab results whose values,
  units and times differ by a little, under the rule list's version 12, and imports a hospital's summary, an exchange's
  that names the hospital as its author, and a clinic's, each holding a potassium of one business identifier, under the
  rule list's version 13. Hana, with the vocabulary's rule list of an app, enters conditions, flu
  shots and lab results whose dates are a year, a month or a time to the hour, under the rule list's version 14. Gus,
  with the vocabulary's rule list of an app, imports his clinic's summaries, whose plan of treatment grows, and the
  hospital course his hospital and his clinic each send, under the rule list's version 17. Gus, with the drug products
  table and the vocabulary's rule list of an app, enters two courses of metformin years apart and a copy within the
  first, under the rule list's version 18.

  Rule: M1. A step's records are those with at least one first revision from that step's import, entry session or connect, the servers its connect wrote any revision for, or all from acts its sync names
    decisions/republishing-sources.md, row "what a run compares"; decisions/performance.md, row "runs bound to arrivals, scoped within an organisation".
    A record's first revisions are those with no prov:wasRevisionOf; it has one or more, one from each writer that
    imported one export. A step's records are those with at least one first revision generated by
    (prov:wasGeneratedBy) the import, entry session or connect that step made, or, for a step that received another writer's
    files, those all of whose first revisions were generated by acts its sync names (prov:wasInformedBy, S2): the
    records first held through that sync; a sync naming no act has none. A server's record (M2) is the step's too when
    any of its revisions, its first or a later one, was generated by the connect that step made, so a reconnect is
    taken (M20). The matcher is given that activity, the sync for a sync, and its run is prov:wasInformedBy it, its cause; it reads no file name and no list of steps. A record's
    arrival is its earliest first revision's prov:generatedAtTime. A record the matcher takes (M2) with no first revision
    fails the run. A run compares
    only its step's records, each with every other record the matcher takes: each comparison query is evaluated with
    ?record bound to the step's records (M4), never over every pair in the pod.

    Two runs compare the whole pod, with ?record unbound, and no other: a recheck (M7), which follows a new version of a
    table or of the rule list, and an open (O1), which then takes the records of the pairs its tables newly join.

    No example can tell this from taking the records whose first revision's file the step wrote, since in a pod that
    follows these rules the two always agree. Each runtime tests it itself.

    Example: a run takes only the records its step brought, so the import's two codeine records are never judged together
      Given a new pod for Cleo on 2026-03-01 at 09:00
      And Cleo files the judgment "about-p" on 2026-03-01 at 09:05
      And the export "first-export" is imported on 2026-03-02 at 10:00
      And Cleo enters "entry" on 2026-03-03 at 08:00
      When the matcher runs on the records of that entry on 2026-03-03 at 08:01
      Then that step wrote these matcher judgments:
        | justification    | members                                                                                                         |
        | same code        | allergy RxNorm 10180 from sulfa, allergy RxNorm 10180 "Gantanol"                                                |
        | same code        | allergy RxNorm 1191 "Aspirin", allergy RxNorm 1191 "Bayer aspirin"                                              |
        | same code        | allergy RxNorm 1191 "Bayer aspirin", allergy RxNorm 1191 "Ecotrin"                                              |
        | same mapped code | allergy RxNorm 7980 "Penicillin G", allergy SNOMED 373270004                                                    |
        | same code        | allergy RxNorm 7980 "Penicillin G", allergy RxNorm 7980 "Bicillin"                                              |
        | same mapped code | allergy RxNorm 7980 "Bicillin", allergy SNOMED 373270004                                                        |
        | same code        | allergy RxNorm 2670 from codeine-1, allergy RxNorm 2670 from codeine-2, allergy RxNorm 2670 "Codeine phosphate" |

    Example: a sync's run takes the records the sync brought, so it joins a pair neither writer held together
      Dev's first writer enters Principen, and the second Ampicillin, both RxNorm 733; the second writer receives the
      first's files.

      Given a new pod for Dev on 2026-04-01 at 09:00
      And a second writer of the pod
      And Dev enters "later" on 2026-04-04 at 09:00
      And the steps that follow are the second writer's
      And Dev enters "pairs" on 2026-04-04 at 10:00
      And the second writer receives the files of the first writer on 2026-04-05 at 09:00
      When the matcher runs on the records of that step on 2026-04-05 at 09:05
      Then the matcher's judgments holding allergy RxNorm 733 "Principen" and allergy RxNorm 733 "Ampicillin" are:
        | justification | members                                                         |
        | same code     | allergy RxNorm 733 "Principen", allergy RxNorm 733 "Ampicillin" |
      When the query is:
        """
        SELECT ?causeStep WHERE {
          GRAPH <urn:cascade:steps> { <urn:cascade:step:matcher> prov:generated ?file . ?causeStep prov:generated ?causeFile }
          GRAPH ?file { ?run a cascade:MatcherRun ; prov:wasInformedBy ?cause }
          GRAPH ?causeFile { ?cause a cascade:SyncFromAnotherWriter }
        }
        """
      Then it answers:
        | causeStep               |
        | <urn:cascade:step:sync> |

  Rule: M2. The matcher takes only the subject's records, and the servers' records that have a version
    decisions/republishing-sources.md, rows "server" and "organisation".
    Those queries/derivations/subjects.rq gives the pod's cascade:Subject, under the everyday lens. A server's or an
    organization's record names no patient, as cascade:ServerShape and cascade:OrganizationShape hold, so it is never
    the subject's (decisions/republishing-sources.md, rows "server" and "organisation"). A server's record is taken all
    the same when it has a version, which only a connect writes (A16), and only R14 applies to it (M20,
    decisions/republishing-sources.md, row "two servers are one"); an organization's record is never taken.

    Example: a record whose patient no About claims, or which names no patient, is never taken nor compared
      Given a new pod for Cleo on 2026-03-01 at 09:00
      And Cleo files the judgment "about-p" on 2026-03-01 at 09:05
      And the export "first-export" is imported on 2026-03-02 at 10:00
      And Cleo enters "entry" on 2026-03-03 at 08:00
      When the matcher runs on the records of that entry on 2026-03-03 at 08:01
      Then the matcher has no judgment holding allergy RxNorm 10180 from sulfa-other, allergy RxNorm 10180 "Septra" and allergy RxNorm 10180 "Bactrim"

    Example: while an About claims a profile the matcher takes its records, and once it is retracted, none
      Given a new pod for Cleo on 2026-03-01 at 09:00
      And Cleo files the judgment "about-r" on 2026-03-04 at 07:00
      And Cleo enters "second-entry" on 2026-03-04 at 08:00
      And the matcher runs on the records of that entry on 2026-03-04 at 08:01
      And Cleo files the judgment "retract-about-r" on 2026-03-04 at 12:00
      And Cleo enters "third-entry" on 2026-03-05 at 08:00
      When the matcher runs on the records of that entry on 2026-03-05 at 08:01 (match-third-entry)
      Then the matcher's judgments holding allergy RxNorm 8591 "Latex gloves", allergy RxNorm 8591 "Latex" and allergy RxNorm 8591 "Natural rubber latex" are:
        | justification | members                                                         |
        | same code     | allergy RxNorm 8591 "Latex gloves", allergy RxNorm 8591 "Latex" |

  Rule: M3. It takes them in order of arrival, then by record name
    No note rules this.
    Arrival is the earliest first revision's prov:generatedAtTime (M1), compared as a time. All of one step's records arrive at the
    step's start (N4), so the record name decides among them. It compares each with the other records it takes, then
    adds it to them for the next.

    No example can tell ordering by record name from ordering by file path, since a record's file is named from its name
    (N9) in its kind's folder and no comparison joins two kinds; this example pins what the order does.

    Example: three records of one code are two judgments, each record compared with those taken before it
      Given a new pod for Cleo on 2026-03-01 at 09:00
      And Cleo enters "entry" on 2026-03-03 at 08:00
      When the matcher runs on the records of that entry on 2026-03-03 at 08:01
      Then the matcher's judgments holding allergy RxNorm 1191 "Aspirin", allergy RxNorm 1191 "Bayer aspirin" and allergy RxNorm 1191 "Ecotrin" are:
        | justification | members                                                                                           |
        | same code     | allergy RxNorm 1191 "Aspirin", allergy RxNorm 1191 "Bayer aspirin"                                |
        | same code     | allergy RxNorm 1191 "Bayer aspirin", allergy RxNorm 1191 "Ecotrin"                                |

  Rule: M4. Each row of the rule list runs its comparison query
    decisions/code-iris.md, row "a literal joining as an IRI", decisions/one-meaning-per-term.md, row "6. `vaccineCode`",
    and decisions/performance.md, row "runs bound to arrivals, scoped within an organisation".
    For each row of the current rule list, the matcher runs the query the row names, with ?record bound to the records
    the run compares (M1), over the pod, its derived state under the everyday lens, and, for a row naming a table kind
    (cascade:tableKind), the rows of the current version of every series of that kind (M13). A row (record, other) of
    its answer, with the version whose rows joined it as ?origin when the rule reads a table, is a match when the
    record's class is one the row applies to (cascade:appliesToClass). A recheck and an open run it with ?record unbound.

    ?record is bound as SPARQL's substitution binds a variable: an initial binding, as pyoxigraph's substitutions and
    rdflib's initBindings give it, one evaluation per record; or, on an engine that takes no initial binding, as
    Oxigraph's JavaScript build does not, a VALUES ?record block of the run's records opening the query's outermost
    group and each group the query marks, one evaluation per query per run. A comparison marks a group with a comment
    line, # bound: ?record, with nothing but white space, or WHERE, between it and the group's opening brace, whose
    first token is not SELECT; a number sign inside a string or an IRI opens no comment. It marks only a group inside a
    sub-select that binds ?record in a triple pattern of its own, outside any nested pattern, and that has no LIMIT,
    OFFSET or HAVING and groups, if it groups or aggregates at all, by ?record; and only where taking ?record as the
    run's records leaves those records' rows as they are. A comparison that works each side of a pair out in a
    sub-select marks the record's side, and a sub-select in the other side that gives it the keys of the records bound,
    such as their codes, so a run works out its records' side and only the others that share a key, never every record
    in the pod. The two give the same rows because every comparison binds ?record in its outermost group before any
    sub-select or MINUS reads it, and binding a marked group drops no row of the run's records. A query is plain SPARQL,
    which an adapter or an app may run unbound.

    A query reads a table's rows only inside GRAPH ?origin, guarded by ?origin prov:specializationOf/cascade:tableKind
    and its kind. A record's code joins a row by the code's IRI, one form per code system (cascade:CodeSystem in
    cascade.ttl): a code held as an IRI, as a vaccine code is, <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/codes/cvx/141>, joins as
    it is; a code written as a literal is its system's void:uriSpace followed by the literal percent-encoded as SPARQL's
    ENCODE_FOR_URI does (every character but A-Z a-z 0-9 - _ . ~ as % and two upper-case hexadecimal digits of its
    UTF-8), as an HGVS expression, which holds reserved characters, cascade:hgvsCodingDnaExpression
    "NM_000249.4:c.2042C>T", is <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/codes/hgvs/NM_000249.4%3Ac.2042C%3ET>. Every query that
    joins a literal to a stem encodes it. The rationale is decisions/code-iris.md.

    - R1, cascade:SameCodeRule, justified cascade:SameCode: queries/matcher/same-code.rq.
    - R2, cascade:SameCodeAndDateRule, justified cascade:SameCodeAndDate: queries/matcher/same-code-and-date.rq.
    - R3, cascade:SameMappedCodeRule, justified cascade:SameMappedCode: in the stories' lists, queries/matcher/same-mapped-code.rq, with
      cascade:SubstanceIngredients, a kind only the stories fill; in the vocabulary's list from its version 2,
      queries/matcher/same-ingredient.rq, with cascade:ProductIngredients, for allergies only. A claim cites its
      rule, and its assertion the list's version and the hash of the query that version's row for the rule runs.
    - R4, cascade:SameMappedCodeAndDateRule, justified cascade:SameMappedCodeAndDate: queries/matcher/same-mapped-code-and-date.rq, with cascade:VaccineGroups.

    Version 2 of the rule list adds two rows, whose examples are under M12:

    - R5, cascade:SameMedicationCodeRule, justified cascade:SameMedicationCode: queries/matcher/same-medication-code.rq.
    - R6, cascade:SameResultRule, justified cascade:SameResult: queries/matcher/same-result.rq.

    Version 3 of the vocabulary's list adds one row, whose examples are under O1:

    - R7, cascade:SameConvertedCodeRule, justified cascade:SameConvertedCode: queries/matcher/same-converted-code.rq, with cascade:CodeConversions, for
      conditions only. A record coded with a retired code is the same as one coded with the one code it converts to;
      a code split into several has no row, since one judgment holds a record and all it matched.

    Version 4 of the vocabulary's list adds one row, whose examples are under M14 and O1, and takes conditions out of R1:

    - R8, cascade:SameCodeAndPeriodRule, justified cascade:SameCodeAndPeriod: queries/matcher/same-code-and-period.rq, for conditions only. Two conditions
      of one ICD-10-CM or SNOMED CT code are the same when their periods overlap.

    Version 5 of the vocabulary's list changes R7's query, whose example is under O1:

    - R7, cascade:SameConvertedCodeRule, justified cascade:SameConvertedCode: queries/matcher/same-one-to-one-conversion.rq, with cascade:CodeConversions. A
      retired code and its replacement are joined only by a dct:isReplacedBy row whose cardinality is 1:1. A split
      (1:n), a merge (n:1) and an n:n row join nothing, and neither does a row of the earlier form, skos:exactMatch
      with no cardinality, which same-converted-code.rq in versions 3 and 4 reads.

    Version 6 of the vocabulary's list changes R5's query and adds a row, whose examples are under M15:

    - R5, cascade:SameMedicationCodeRule, justified cascade:SameMedicationCode: queries/matcher/same-drug-code.rq. Two medications are the same when they
      share an NDC code or the same chosen RxNorm code: the one their source selected (cascade:userSelectedCode), else their
      only one. It reads no table, and does not check that a shared RxNorm code is a product: that is carried from
      version 5 until jayostis/cascade-vocabulary#121.
    - R10, cascade:SameBrandGenericRule, justified cascade:SameBrandGeneric: queries/matcher/same-brand-generic.rq, with cascade:BrandGenerics, for
      medications only. A branded product and its generic, or two brands of one generic, when the records share no code.

    Version 7 of the vocabulary's list changes R5's query and adds a row, whose examples are under M16:

    - R5, cascade:SameMedicationCodeRule, justified cascade:SameMedicationCode: queries/matcher/same-ndc-code.rq. Two medications that share an NDC code.
      It reads no table.
    - R11, cascade:SameProductCodeRule, justified cascade:SameProductCode: queries/matcher/same-product-code.rq, with cascade:DrugProducts, for medications
      only. Two medications that share an RxNorm code which the held version of a drug products table says names one
      product. A pair that shares an NDC is left to R5.

    Version 8 of the vocabulary's list adds two rows, whose examples are under M17:

    - R12, cascade:SameVariationRule, justified cascade:SameVariation: queries/matcher/same-variation.rq, for variants only. Two variants whose
      current versions carry one ClinVar VariationID.
    - R13, cascade:SameVariationThroughTableRule, justified cascade:SameVariationThroughTable: queries/matcher/same-variation-through-table.rq, with
      cascade:VariantClassifications, for variants only. Two variants whose current versions find one row of one version
      of a variant classifications table.

